  "worked_solution": "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-18x+8y & =-13 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-18x-2{y}^{2}+8y & =-13 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}-6x]-2[{y}^{2}-4y] & =-13 \\\\ 3[(x-3)^{2}-9]-2[(y-2)^{2}-4] & =-13 \\\\ 3(x-3)^{2}-27-2(y-2)^{2}+8 & =-13 \\\\ 3(x-3)^{2}-2(y-2)^{2} & =-13+27-8 \\\\ 3(x-3)^{2}-2(y-2)^{2} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{3} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center is $(3,2).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
